2.Related Work
Test-Time Scaling via Sampling and Refinement.Chain-of-Thought prompting (Wei et al.,2023) established the basis for scaling test-time computation through intermediate reasoning. Subsequent methods such as Self-Consistency (Wang et al.,2023) and Best-of-N (Kang et al.,2025) improve accuracy by aggregating sampled trajectories. Iterative refinement further enables self-correction: SelfRefine (Madaan et al.,2023) and Reflexion (Shinn et al.,2023) revise outputs with verbal feedback, while CodeT (Chen et al.,2022) and Self-Debug (Chen et al.,2023) use execution feedback to verify and repair code. Recent systems such as OpenAI o1 (OpenAI,2024) further combine extended reasoning with self-correction. However, these methods operate under afrozen policy: feedback is used only for filtering, rewriting, or prompting, without updating the underlying reasoning strategy.
Structured Search and Planning.Reasoning can also be formulated as structured search. Tree of Thoughts (Yao et al.,2023) and RAP (Hao et al.,2023) search over candidate thoughts, later extended by LATS (Zhou et al.,2024) and planning-based approaches (Zhang et al.,2023). To improve efficiency, ReST-MCTS (Zhang et al.,2024) and process reward-guided ensembling (Park et al.,2024) incorporate finer-grained feedback into Monte Carlo Tree Search. Recent work including AB-MCTS (Inoue et al.,2025) and RethinkMCTS (Li et al.,2025) further improves search efficiency and thought refinement. Despite strong exploration ability, these methods still keep the generation policy fixed throughout search.
Test-Time Training and Adaptation.A growing line of work relaxes the frozen-policy assumption through gradient-based updates at inference.Akyürek et al.(2025) first demonstrated test-time training (TTT) for few-shot learning. This idea has since expanded: TTT-E2E (Tandon et al.,2025) applies TTT to long-context modeling, In-Place TTT (Cai et al.,2026) enables adaptation for Trans
